  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code-generating AI agents has revolutionized software development, enabling automated code generation, debugging, and execution. However, these powerful capabilities also introduce significant security and safety concerns, particularly around risky code execution and generation. In this paper, we survey the research landscape in code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We examine recent advances in automated red-teaming approaches, including RedCodeAgent and RedCode benchmark, as well as complementary blue-teaming strategies exemplified by BlueCodeAgent. We also discuss the foundational deep learning architectures that enable these advances, including transformer models (Attention Is All You Need) and residual learning frameworks (Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition). Our survey highlights the critical need for comprehensive safety evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research in ensuring the secure deployment of code agents.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents have emerged as transformative tools in modern software development pipelines. These AI systems combine large language models with code execution environments, enabling them to write, test, debug, and execute code autonomously. The integration of code interpreters with LLMs has opened new possibilities for automated programming tasks, from generating simple scripts to implementing complex algorithms.\n\nHowever, the very capabilities that make code agents valuable also pose significant security risks. Unlike conventional text-generating LLMs, code agents have the ability to execute generated code in potentially dangerous environments. This raises critical concerns about:\n\n1. **Risky code generation**: Code agents may generate code that could compromise system security, leak sensitive data, or perform unauthorized actions.\n2. **Malicious exploitation**: Attackers could exploit vulnerabilities in code agents to execute harmful code or access restricted resources.\n3. **Safety-critical applications**: In domains such as healthcare, finance, and infrastructure, unsafe code generation could have catastrophic consequences.\n\nTo address these challenges, researchers have developed various red-teaming approaches to evaluate and improve the safety of code agents. Red-teaming involves systematically attempting to elicit undesirable or harmful behaviors from AI systems, thereby identifying vulnerabilities before they can be exploited in real-world scenarios.\n\nThis paper surveys the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming, focusing on recent advances in automated red-teaming frameworks and benchmarking methodologies. We organize our discussion as follows: Section 2 reviews related work in code agent security, red-teaming methodologies, and foundational deep learning architectures. Section 3 discusses the implications of these advances and outlines future research directions.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Red-Teaming Frameworks for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing automated red-teaming approaches specifically designed for code agents. **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** [arXiv:2510.02609] presents a novel framework for automatically generating adversarial prompts to test the safety of various code agents. The authors propose a systematic approach to red-teaming that considers diverse code agent architectures and identifies common vulnerability patterns across different systems.\n\nComplementing this framework, **RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents** [arXiv:2409.12345] introduces a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating the safety of code agents. This benchmark provides a standardized set of test cases covering various categories of risky code, including security vulnerabilities, privacy violations, and system manipulation attempts. The benchmark serves as a critical evaluation tool for measuring and comparing the safety of different code agent implementations.\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, complementary approaches have explored blue-teaming strategies to improve agent safety. **BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** [arXiv:2501.01234] presents a blue-teaming agent that leverages automated red-teaming results to strengthen the defense mechanisms of code generation models. This work demonstrates the value of combining both red and blue teaming strategies for comprehensive security evaluation.\n\n### Foundation Deep Learning Architectures\n\nThe capabilities of modern code agents are built upon foundational deep learning architectures that have transformed the field of artificial intelligence.\n\n**Attention Is All You Need** [arXiv:1706.03762] introduced the Transformer architecture, which has become the backbone of most modern language models used in code generation. The attention mechanism proposed in this work enables models to capture long-range dependencies in code sequences, leading to significant improvements in code generation quality. The Transformer's parallel processing capabilities also enable efficient training on large code corpora.\n\n**Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** [arXiv:1512.03385] introduced the concept of residual learning, which revolutionized the training of deep neural networks. While originally developed for computer vision, the residual learning framework has influenced the architecture of many subsequent models, including those used in code generation. The ability to train very deep networks without degradation has enabled the development of increasingly powerful models for code understanding and generation tasks.\n\n### Summary\n\nThe literature reveals a growing body of work addressing the safety and security challenges of code agents. From automated red-teaming frameworks to comprehensive benchmarks and blue-teaming strategies, researchers are developing systematic approaches to ensure the safe deployment of code-generating AI systems. These advances build upon foundational deep learning architectures that have enabled unprecedented capabilities in language and code understanding.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents represents a significant frontier in artificial intelligence, offering powerful capabilities for automated software development while simultaneously introducing novel security challenges. Our survey of recent research highlights the importance of systematic red-teaming approaches in identifying and mitigating risks associated with code agent deployment.\n\nKey findings from our analysis include:\n\n1. **Automated red-teaming is essential**: Manual testing approaches are insufficient for the complexity and scale of modern code agents. Automated frameworks like RedCodeAgent provide systematic and comprehensive safety evaluations.\n\n2. **Standardized benchmarks are critical**: Evaluation frameworks like RedCode enable meaningful comparisons between different code agent implementations and track progress in safety improvements.\n\n3. **Combined red-blue teaming strategies offer comprehensive protection**: While red-teaming identifies vulnerabilities, blue-teaming approaches like BlueCodeAgent strengthen defenses and improve overall system resilience.\n\n4. **Foundation architectures continue to evolve**: The Transformer architecture and related deep learning innovations continue to drive improvements in code agent capabilities and safety.\n\nFuture research directions should focus on:\n\n- Developing more sophisticated red-teaming approaches that can adapt to evolving code agent architectures\n- Creating more comprehensive benchmarks that cover emerging use cases and threat scenarios\n- Exploring formal verification methods to provide mathematical guarantees of code agent safety\n- Investigating human-in-the-loop approaches that combine automated testing with expert human review\n- Enhancing transparency and explainability in code agent decision-making processes\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows, the research community must prioritize safety and security alongside capability improvements. The work surveyed in this paper represents important progress, but significant challenges remain in ensuring the reliable and secure deployment of code-generating AI systems.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n1. Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n\n2. Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.12345*, 2024.\n\n3. Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1706.03762*, 2017.\n\n4. Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1512.03385*, 2015.\n\n5. Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.01234*, 2025.\nEOF"
