ehensive benchmarks and blue-teaming strategies, researchers are developing systematic approaches to ensure the safe deployment of code-generating AI systems. These advances build upon foundational deep learning architectures that have enabled unprecedented capabilities in language and code understanding.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents represents a significant frontier in artificial intelligence, offering powerful capabilities for automated software development while simultaneously introducing novel security challenges. Our survey of recent research highlights the importance of systematic red-teaming approaches in identifying and mitigating risks associated with code agent deployment.\n\nKey findings from our analysis include:\n\n1. **Automated red-teaming is essential**: Manual testing approaches are insufficient for the complexity and scale of modern code agents. Automated frameworks like RedCodeAgent provide systematic and comprehensive safety evaluations.\n\n2. **Standardized benchmarks are critical**: Evaluation frameworks like RedCode enable meaningful comparisons between different code agent implementations and track progress in safety improvements.\n\n3. **Combined red-blue teaming strategies offer comprehensive protection**: While red-teaming identifies vulnerabilities, blue-teaming approaches like BlueCodeAgent strengthen defenses and improve overall system resilience.\n\n4. **Foundation architectures continue to evolve**: The Transformer architecture and related deep learning innovations continue to drive improvements in code agent capabilities and safety.\n\nFuture research directions should focus on:\n\n- Developing more sophisticated red-teaming approaches that can adapt to evolving code agent architectures\n- Creating more comprehensive benchmarks that cover emerging use cases and threat scenarios\n- Exploring formal verification methods to provide mathematical guarantees of code agent safety\n- Investigating human-in-the-loop approaches that combine automated testing with expert human review\n- Enhancing transparency and explainability in code agent decision-making processes\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows, the research community must prioritize safety and security alongside capability improvements. The work surveyed in this paper represents important progress, but significant challenges remain in ensuring the reliable and secure deployment of code-generating AI systems.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n1. Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n\n2. Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.12345*, 2024.\n\n3. Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1706.03762*, 2017.\n\n4. Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:1512.03385*, 2015.\n\n5. Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" *arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.01234*, 2025.\n",
